Cell["\<\
In actuality, it seems like the oscillation stabilizes with an average \
slightly larger than the average of the two end pressures (see Jacob Figure \
4.23 and Nate Fig 4). I think we will need to figure out what that \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]stable oscillation pressure\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
is? --- This is purely just due to the fact that the temperature/density is \
different at two ends of the valves. And the stable point is the point with \
zero mass flow. \[LineSeparator]\
\>", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.964797394197227*^9, 3.964797465335281*^9}, {
  3.964797522310577*^9, 3.9647975570463963`*^9}, {3.9666150090360622`*^9, 
  3.966615089722212*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ad906bc4-af36-41ed-850a-\
fae04c21c969"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Now, I want to check that, what is the thing that drives the initial increase \
in oscillation. For this I need the valves overlapping each other (i think). \
\
\>", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9596914085900097`*^9, {3.959691449165729*^9, 3.9596914940426493`*^9}, 
   3.9596915270452433`*^9, {3.9596915600604773`*^9, 3.959691621892503*^9}, 
   3.959691661236623*^9, {3.960404545302371*^9, 3.960404563330387*^9}, {
   3.960404669027145*^9, 
   3.960404682408807*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"90448977-53d4-4ec6-8951-\
459890c5314c"],

